editorial:
For [2,9,4], index 3 is the second greater position for index 1 because both 9 and 4 exceed 2. Keep indices waiting for their first hit and indices waiting for their second hit, each with nonincreasing values from bottom to top. A new value resolves all smaller tops in the second stack. Then move all smaller tops from the first stack to the second in reverse pop order. This restores nonincreasing order: existing tops are at least the current value, and moved values are smaller. Resolving the second stack first ensures a position contributes at most one hit to any index. Each index is pushed and popped a constant number of times, for O(n) time and space.
~~~
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    std::cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    std::cin >> n;
    std::vector<int> a(n), answer(n), waitingFirst, waitingSecond, moved;
    for (int& x : a) std::cin >> x;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        while (!waitingSecond.empty() && a[waitingSecond.back()] < a[i]) {
            answer[waitingSecond.back()] = i + 1;
            waitingSecond.pop_back();
        }
        while (!waitingFirst.empty() && a[waitingFirst.back()] < a[i]) {
            moved.push_back(waitingFirst.back());
            waitingFirst.pop_back();
        }
        while (!moved.empty()) {
            waitingSecond.push_back(moved.back());
            moved.pop_back();
        }
        waitingFirst.push_back(i);
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) std::cout << answer[i] << (i + 1 == n ? '\n' : ' ');
}
